<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=quipu">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quipu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600–<a href="/years/1000">1000 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Khipu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A quipu, or khipu, is a record-keeping device of knotted cords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> or camelid fibre, first undisputedly attested in the central Andes in the Middle Horizon, c. 600–1000 CE, when it was probably used by the Wari Empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> stored numbers in its knots in a decimal positional system and used quipus for taxes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> and the calendar. How quipus recorded non-numeric information remains undeciphered and disputed; just under 1,400 pre-Columbian examples are known.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quipu.jpg" alt="Quipu" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lyndsaruell at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inca_Quipu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Inca officials recorded census figures, tribute and stored goods on <b>khipus</b>, knotted cords whose knot types and positions encoded numbers in a base-ten system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Wari had no writing, but cords with knots and coloured wrappings, early forms of the <b>khipus</b> later used by the Incas, have been radiocarbon-dated to between the late eighth and early eleventh centuries, and a 2024 study of some 370 radiocarbon dates suggests that such record-keeping and roads…</blockquote><a href="/empire/wari-empire">Wari Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They had no writing in the usual sense; their officials kept census, tribute and calendar records on <b>khipus</b>, cords of coloured and knotted strings, which recorded numbers in a decimal system and perhaps other information, a question still studied today.</blockquote><a href="/language/quechua">Quechua · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A knotted string found at Caral has been called a <b>quipu</b>, the recording device later perfected by the Inca; it has not been formally published and remains undeciphered, but its excavators take it as evidence that record-keeping with knots is older than had been thought.</blockquote><a href="/culture/caral-supe-civilisation">Caral–Supe civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The Inca kept their records on <b>khipus</b>, knotted cords; the Nahuas painted codices whose glyphs could be read as sounds; the Hawaiians had no writing apart from petroglyphs, and kept their history in stories and in hula.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>The Incas had no writing, only knotted-cord accounts (<b>khipu</b>), so the evidence comes from archaeology and from chroniclers writing after the conquest: Pedro Cieza de León, Juan de Betanzos, Garcilaso de la Vega and Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 600–1000 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The central Andes</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/wari-empire">Wari Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A possible proto-quipu from Caral, of 3000–1800 BCE, has yet to be evaluated.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=quipu">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
